statement:
Let $X$ be a scheme and $G$ a sheaf of sets on $X_{\Proet}$. The following are
equivalent:

1. $G\cong\nu_X^*F$ for some sheaf $F$ on $X_{\Et}$;
2. $G$ is classical in the sense of [det-pe-classical-definition-continuity-map];
3. the counit $\varepsilon_G\colon\nu_X^*\nu_{X,*}G\to G$ is an isomorphism.

proof.text:
$(1)\Rightarrow(2)$. Choose an isomorphism $\alpha:G\xrightarrow{\sim}
u_X^*F$ and any pro-étale affine presentation $U=\varprojlim_iU_i$ from [det-pe-classical-definition-continuity-map]. The scheme-general continuity theorem [lem-weil-pbc-proetale-classical-continuity-sets] applied to this diagram says that its canonical restriction cocone for $
u_X^*F$ is a colimit: $c_{
u_X^*F,U}:\varinjlim_i(
u_X^*F)(U_i)	o(
u_X^*F)(U)$ is bijective. Naturality of $\alpha$ gives $\alpha_U\circ c_{G,U}=c_{
u_X^*F,U}\circ(\varinjlim_i\alpha_{U_i})$. Both $\alpha_U$ and the colimit map induced by the $\alpha_{U_i}$ are bijective; the inverse of the latter is induced by $\alpha^{-1}$. Thus $c_{G,U}$ is bijective. This holds for every presentation, so $G$ is classical.
